@meta
id: 16-11
title: Side by side
chapter: 16
type: lesson
skills: split-window, window-nav
par: 120
minutes: 6
vscode: drag the tab to the right half of the editor
open: payment.service.ts

@brief
`:sp` stacks windows. `:vs` puts them side by side, which is the better
shape for code: lines are wide and screens are wider. `:vs errors.json`
opens the errors file in a new window on the left, cursor in it, with
the service still on the right. `Ctrl-W l` moves right, `Ctrl-W h` moves
left, `Ctrl-W w` cycles to the next window whichever way it is.

The payment service is the job. It throws `REFUND_DECLINED` on a 402,
and that code does not exist yet; it also throws `REFUND_FAILD`, which
is a typo for a code that does. With the two files beside each other
both problems are visible at once.

  1. `errors.json`: add a line directly above `REFUND_FAILED`:

         "REFUND_DECLINED": { "statusCode": 402, "sentry": false },

  2. `payment.service.ts`: `REFUND_FAILD` becomes `REFUND_FAILED`.

@keys
| `:vs name` | open `name` in a new window to the left |
| `:vs` | split this window vertically, same buffer in both |
| `Ctrl-W v` | the same, as a chord |
| `Ctrl-W h` `Ctrl-W l` | window to the left, window to the right |
| `Ctrl-W w` | next window, wrapping round; `Ctrl-W p` the previous one |

@hints
`:vs errors.json`. You are in the left window. `/FAILED`, `O` to open a
line above, the new entry, `Esc`.
--
`Ctrl-W l` puts you in the service. `/FAILD`, then `cw` replaces
`FAILD` because the cursor is on its first letter and the word ends at
the quote.
--
`:wqa` writes both and closes both windows.

@solution
    :vs errors.json<Enter>  /FAILED<Enter>  O "REFUND_DECLINED": { "statusCode": 402, "sentry": false }, <Esc>
    Ctrl-W l  /FAILD<Enter>  cw FAILED <Esc>  :wqa<Enter>

About a hundred and five keystrokes; the JSON line is sixty of them.

Two files side by side is the layout for almost every cross-file edit:
the type and the code that uses it, the error table and the throw, the
test and the thing under test. `:vs name` builds it in one command
from wherever you are, and you did not have to leave the service or
put it in the buffer list first; it was there when you came back.

Which side the new window lands on is the `splitright` option, off by
default, so new vertical windows open on the left. Many people set it
in their vimrc because a new window on the right feels more natural.
Chapter 27 is where you decide.

@start payment.service.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

@injectable()
export class PaymentService {
  public async refund(orderId: string) {
    const response = await this.http.post(`/refunds`, { orderId });

    if (response.status === 402) {
      throw exception('REFUND_DECLINED');
    }

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('REFUND_FAILD');
    }

    return response.data;
  }
}

@expected payment.service.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

@injectable()
export class PaymentService {
  public async refund(orderId: string) {
    const response = await this.http.post(`/refunds`, { orderId });

    if (response.status === 402) {
      throw exception('REFUND_DECLINED');
    }

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('REFUND_FAILED');
    }

    return response.data;
  }
}

@start errors.json
{
  "ORDER_NOT_FOUND": { "statusCode": 404, "sentry": false },
  "ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED": { "statusCode": 409, "sentry": false },
  "CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND": { "statusCode": 404, "sentry": false },
  "REFUND_FAILED": { "statusCode": 502, "sentry": true }
}

@expected errors.json
{
  "ORDER_NOT_FOUND": { "statusCode": 404, "sentry": false },
  "ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED": { "statusCode": 409, "sentry": false },
  "CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND": { "statusCode": 404, "sentry": false },
  "REFUND_DECLINED": { "statusCode": 402, "sentry": false },
  "REFUND_FAILED": { "statusCode": 502, "sentry": true }
}
